-- Concurrent File Search Benchmark — CLEAR Reference Design
-- @use_zig
--
-- WHAT THIS MEASURES:
--
--   Opens all files in a directory, counts occurrences of a needle string
--   in each file concurrently using fibers, then sorts files by count.
--
--   CLEAR (bench.zig): One fiber per file via Promise(i64) + CLEAR scheduler.
--                      Cooperative multitasking: fibers run one at a time but
--                      yield at checkYield() boundaries (~4096 iterations).
--                      All 128 fibers are in-flight simultaneously; each
--                      completes its file read without blocking others.
--
--   Go (bench.go):     One goroutine per file. Go runtime uses GOMAXPROCS
--                      (default = num_cpu) for true parallelism.
--
--   Rust (bench.rs):   One OS thread per file via std::thread::spawn.
--                      True OS-level parallelism; higher per-task overhead.
--
-- KEY COMPARISON:
--   - Spawn overhead:  CLEAR fibers (~2KB stack) < Go goroutines (~8KB) < OS threads (8MB)
--   - Concurrency:     CLEAR = cooperative M:1  |  Go = M:N  |  Rust = 1:1
--   - Memory safety:   All three guarantee safety (CLEAR via arenas/ownership)
--
-- NOTE: bench.cht is the conceptual CLEAR design. bench.zig is the actual
-- compiled version (hand-written Zig using CLEAR's scheduler/runtime API)
-- because CLEAR does not yet support loop-based dynamic BG spawning.
-- The .zig file uses the same CLEAR runtime infrastructure (scheduler, arenas,
-- Promise(T), WaitGroup) that transpiled CLEAR code uses.
--
-- FUTURE CLEAR DESIGN (when loop-based spawning is supported):
--
--   FN countFile(path: String, needle: String) RETURNS Int64 ->
--       content = readFile(path);
--       RETURN countOccurrences(content, needle);
--   END
--
--   FN cheatMain() RETURNS Void ->
--       needle = "the";
--       files = listDir("benchmarks/10_concurrent_search/data");
--
--       -- Spawn one fiber per file (future loop-based BG syntax):
--       -- results: ~Int64[] = files.map(|f| BG { countFile(f, needle) });
--       -- CLEAR would automatically collect and sort results.
--
--       -- Current workaround: use BoundedStream for fixed N, or bench.zig
--
--       RETURN;
--   END
--
-- DATASET:
--   N = 128 files × ~10 KB each = ~1.28 MB total
--   Needle: "the" (common, ~200-300 occurrences per file)
--   Generated deterministically from a hash seed (reproducible).
--
-- COMPILE (handled by benchmarks/runner.rb):
--   CLEAR:  zig build-exe zig/bench.zig zig/switch.S zig/onRoot.S \
--               --name bench_clear -O ReleaseFast -lc
--   Go:     cd benchmarks/10_concurrent_search && go build -o bench_go .
--   Rust:   rustc -C opt-level=3 bench.rs -o bench_rust
